# Vala Null Checking Best Practices

## When Null Checks Are Unnecessary

In Vala, if a property or variable is **not** defined as nullable (doesn't have `?`), then it **cannot be null** and null checks are pointless and should be avoided.

### ❌ Incorrect - Unnecessary null checks

```vala
// Property is NOT nullable - cannot be null
public string name { get; set; default = ""; }

// This null check is pointless and should be removed
if (this.name != null) {
    // This code will always execute
    doSomething(this.name);
}

// This is also pointless
if (this.name == null) {
    // This code will never execute
    return;
}
```

### ✅ Correct - No null checks needed

```vala
// Property is NOT nullable - cannot be null
public string name { get; set; default = ""; }

// Direct usage without null checks
doSomething(this.name);

// Or with default value handling
var displayName = this.name.length > 0 ? this.name : "Unnamed";
```

## When Null Checks Are Required

Only check for null when the property or variable is **nullable** (has `?`).

### ✅ Correct - Null checks for nullable types

```vala
// Property IS nullable - can be null
public string? description { get; set; default = null; }

// Null check is necessary and correct
if (this.description != null) {
    doSomething(this.description);
}

// Or use null coalescing
var displayDesc = this.description ?? "No description";
```

## Common Patterns

### Object Properties
```vala
// Non-nullable - no null check needed
public Node parent { get; set; }

// Direct usage
this.parent.updateCount++;

// Nullable - null check required
public Node? child { get; set; default = null; }

// Proper null checking
if (this.child != null) {
    this.child.updateCount++;
}
```

### Method Parameters
```vala
// Non-nullable parameter - no null check needed
public void processNode(Node node) {
    node.updateCount++; // Safe to use directly
}

// Nullable parameter - null check required
public void processOptionalNode(Node? node) {
    if (node != null) {
        node.updateCount++;
    }
}
```

## Performance Impact

Unnecessary null checks:
- Add runtime overhead
- Clutter the code
- Can confuse other developers
- May indicate misunderstanding of Vala's type system

## Code Review Guidelines

When reviewing Vala code, look for:
1. Null checks on non-nullable types (remove them)
2. Missing null checks on nullable types (add them)
3. Consistent use of null coalescing (`??`) for nullable types
4. Proper handling of optional parameters and return values

## Examples from This Codebase

```vala
// From Node.vala - prop_type is non-nullable
protected string prop_type { get; set; default = ""; }

// ❌ Don't do this
if (this.prop_type != null && this.prop_type.contains(".")) {
    // ...
}

// ✅ Do this instead
if (this.prop_type.contains(".")) {
    // ...
}
```

Remember: Vala's type system prevents null access violations at compile time for non-nullable types, so trust the compiler and avoid unnecessary null checks.